# config/Kconfig.brain — Brain server link defaults and subsumption layer enables
#
# RFC-0026 Phase C1.  Compile-time defaults for the brain link.
# Runtime values can be overridden in CONFIG.INI.
#
# Sourced from inside the Robot menu (config/Kconfig.robot), so it is shown
# only for DOMAIN_ROBOT. The brain-link task is compiled only into a
# DOMAIN_ROBOT image (kernel feature `domain-robot`, wave 11); its two
# enforcement switches in "Security" depend on DOMAIN_ROBOT.

menu "Brain Server Link"
config MULTISTREAM_SCHED_PRIORITY
    bool "Multi-stream scheduler: priority (control preempts bulk)"
    default n
    help
      RFC-0021 multi-stream link scheduling policy — a compile-time
      choice so each binary carries exactly one policy (zero hot-path
      overhead; the unused branch is const-eliminated).

      n (default) = FIFO: streams share the link in send order; a large
        STREAM_CAMERA frame can head-of-line-block a STREAM_CONTROL frame.
      y = PRIORITY: STREAM_CONTROL frames are interleaved ahead of bulk
        stream chunks, bounding control hold-off to ~one chunk-time
        instead of one full bulk-frame-time (experiment I2).

      Selected via `make menuconfig` / defconfig; surfaces as
      `azos_limits::MULTISTREAM_SCHED_PRIORITY`. A/B the two builds
      with the bench_boot capture + dashboard. Outside DOMAIN_ROBOT the
      option is hidden and reads as n (FIFO).

config BRAIN_TX_QUEUE_BYTES
    int "Brain link: control-socket transmit queue (bytes)"
    range 4096 262144
    default 12288 if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 32768 if PROFILE_FLEET
    default 16384
    help
      Wave 15 (IO-QUEUES-AUDIT B1). The behavior task seals each message
      for the brain into this queue and returns; the `brain-tx` task
      (BRAIN_TX_PRIORITY) puts it on the socket, waiting for the TCP window
      there and not in the subsumption loop. One FIFO of sealed wire bytes
      (RFC-0019 records cannot be dropped or reordered once sealed).

      Costs this many bytes of .bss. A camera frame sent on the control
      connection (no `behavior_camera_port`) is about 8.5 KiB and is only
      admitted with that much room above BRAIN_TX_CONTROL_RESERVE_BYTES;
      below ~10 KiB the control connection carries no camera frames.
      Raise it for a slow or lossy link where telemetry frames are dropped
      (the `[BRAIN-TX]` counters), lower it on a RAM-tight board that sends
      no camera frames on this connection.

config BRAIN_TX_CONTROL_RESERVE_BYTES
    int "Brain link: queue bytes only control messages may use"
    range 256 65536
    default 1024
    help
      Telemetry (sensor and camera frames) is admitted only while this much
      of BRAIN_TX_QUEUE_BYTES stays free, so control messages (the status
      packet, an RFC-0019 REJECT) are never refused because telemetry
      filled the queue. A refused telemetry frame is dropped before it is
      sealed and counted. No RAM of its own. Raise it if control messages
      grow beyond ~1 KiB sealed.

config BRAIN_TX_PRIORITY
    int "Brain link: priority of the brain-tx sender task"
    range 12 30
    default 15
    help
      The task that dials the brain for the behavior task (DHCP, connect,
      the RFC-0019 handshake) and drains BRAIN_TX_QUEUE_BYTES to the
      socket. Below the behavior task (14) on the same hart, so a send or a
      dial never delays the subsumption loop; above the default priority
      (16) so the queue does not wait behind background work. Not in the
      RT band (12 and below): it waits on the network.

config BRAIN_TX_IDLE_PARK_MS
    int "Brain link: brain-tx park with an empty queue (ms)"
    range 10 10000
    default 100
    help
      How long the sender sleeps when nothing is queued. The behavior task
      wakes it at the end of each tick that queued bytes, so this only
      bounds the delay of a lost wake. One timer wake per period.

config BRAIN_TX_STALL_MS
    int "Brain link: end the session after the socket takes nothing (ms)"
    range 1100 60000
    default 2000
    help
      Queued bytes the socket has taken none of for this long end the
      session (fail closed); the next tick dials a fresh handshake. Above
      one TCP retransmission timeout (1000 ms) so one lost segment does not
      end a healthy session; below the brain's shortest comms timeout
      (3 s, drone) so the brain does not declare the link lost first.

config BRAIN_SEND_BUDGET_US
    int "Brain link: longest blocking send (us)"
    range 10000 5000000
    default 500000
    help
      Bound of the blocking sends left outside the queue: the RFC-0019
      handshake's frames (HELLO reply, REJECT), sent by the brain-tx task
      while it dials (the behavior task never waits for them). The order of
      the motor watchdog (500 ms without a command SAFE-STOPs).

config BRAIN_NET_WAIT_POLL_MS
    int "Brain link: network wait poll period (ms)"
    range 1 100
    default 1
    help
      The sleep between polls of a brain-link network wait (handshake
      frames, ARP, the sender's closed TCP window). Shorter: lower latency
      after the event, more timer wakes (one per period while waiting).

endmenu # Brain Server Link
